and  kassim sheikh batsman 27 times capped  for your country casum  this started in scotland with you with  yourself and magic going public about  your experiences  uh within cricket  tell me about the racism that you  experienced  i think um it all started obviously when  nazim rafiq  spoke out south of the border  and then we were asked questions  in scotland of what we felt  and what we felt was you know we were  always treated  differently  you just have to look at kind of the  stats quite easily to  compare how many minorities have played  for the national team and we were we  were lucky to make it to that point but  you know stumbled and didn't play after  speaking out against a coach at the time  so over reaction magic sent a tweet in  2015 sent home from the world cup never  to play for his country again i never  played again  these type of overreactions uh suggest  to me that we're treated differently  because of of the color of our skin  compared to to white counterparts which  i don't feel they face the same thing  in terms of participation in cricket um  we're at the edinburgh south club here  at inch in edinburgh  there is huge participation  among south asians  at this level but relatively few at the  top level  we had this conversation before we went  before we went on and i said have a look  around this is just one one small  cricket club in scotland and what 90  percent of the the kids here are  probably from south asian minority  backgrounds um it's the same across the  country so yes why doesn't add up you  know a national team level i'm not  suggesting we have quota systems or  anything like that not at all i just  wanted to see equal opportunities  and people from minorities have got a  south asian background in particular  crickets in the blood um i would like to  see more of them coming through and  playing at national team level there is  reasonable representation at junior  level but it's the jump from there to  national level that seems that just  doesn't add up for me and you believe  that racism cut your cricket career  short  yes i do believe the overreaction i i  publicly apologized as well i did  you know a journalist was chosen for me  to speak to to apologize i did the  apology and the the article was titled  shake it's humble pie um did i ever play  for my country again no so  yes i do think race was part of that  reason tell me about the impact on  yourself your family your life  yeah playing for scotland was was  everything to me um i traveled the world  to better myself as a player i played  from the age of 12 for scotland um all  through the age groups when i got to  national team level it just didn't seem  to i didn't seem to set to get  comfortable there at any point or feel i  had the same opportunities as others  it's affected my affected my my mental  health i was definitely going through  depression for the two three years after  playing um this will probably never  leave me i'm not going to go back in a  cricket pitch for scotland again so i'll  probably be thinking about this for for  the rest of my days that i had  unfinished business  but coming on 38 now what's important to  me is that the next generation current  generation are treated fairly and equal  opportunities are given you've not told  us details of what's in this report  that's something we've learned elsewhere  but following publication on monday what  chains do you want to see made  i want to see more participation  from the minorities  at board level um administration  administrators coaches players  for me it's top to bottom and even  cricket clubs as well i feel like having  minorities involved and their um and  their boards etc and their committees  i'd like to see more people  getting involved and uh yeah definitely  participation higher numbers is  important very much so who would you  blame or what would you believe is it  the structure overall or individuals  concerned  within qriket scotland i think a lot of  the buck falls with the leaders you know  boardroom level these are the one people  that really need to answer the question  they're the ones that should be  setting the right  tone and cricket and that should be  filtering through all the way down to  grassroots so yes i think um the the  board level is where the questions need  to be asked  thanks very much indeed for joining us  cassie your captain of edinburgh south  these days yeah no i'm player coach  player coach uh pretty much like  captaincy uh we've got a big game today  and tomorrow against the same team so  yeah  nice to  nice to looking for hopefully the rain  stays off your colleague tells me  there's going to be some rain in the air  good luck with that thank you thanks for  watching dude for your time uh cassie  shane one of the the two individuals at  the heart of this matter qriket scotland  have issued a statement and i'll read it  to you they have said in response to our  reporting cricket should be a welcoming  place for everyone and not some whether  racism or any form of discrimination  takes place  the cricket scotland board they say is  truly sorry to everyone who has  experienced racism and cricket in  scotland they go on to say we would like  to sincerely thank everyone who's been  involved in contributing to the review  however the board is not in a position  to comment on the independent reviews  report as we will not see it until it's  published publicly  on monday the statement from qriket  scotland
 

